    // function's name in the CURRENT scope. Unlike a top-level function (invokeBody
    // resets to the global scope), a local function captures its defining scope like
    // a lambda, so it sees the enclosing locals; the name is declared before the body
    // captures it, so it can recurse. `this` stays whatever the enclosing scope holds.
    // A local class/interface/object statement. The descriptor was built while walking;
    // running the statement is what puts the name in the current frame.
    // The index operator's argument list. Kotlin's `a[i, j]` is `get(i, j)`; only the
